      "id": 1,
      "text": "Escandón began as part of the 19th‑century Hacienda de la Condesa, later split into lots in the 1880s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        20,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escand%C3%B3n"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The colonia divides into Escandón I, bounded by Patriotismo Avenue and Tacubaya, and Escandón II, extending to Viaducto Miguel Alemán.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escand%C3%B3n"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Escandón I now buzzes with cafés, contemporary galleries, and colorful street art that showcase its cultural revival.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://perfectitinerary.io/mexico/mexico-city/neighborhoods/escandon-i-seccion"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Line 9 of the Mexico City Metro serves Escandón with Patriotismo and Centro Médico stations, putting the area within easy reach of downtown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escand%C3%B3n"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Ecobici bike network expanded in 2011 and 2015, adding several stations that now line the neighborhood’s main avenues.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escand%C3%B3n"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Escandón’s streets honor Mexican history, naming avenues after dates like 11 de Abril and figures such as Salvador Alvarado.",
